# Scan-time artifacts. The IR is regenerated from source every run and is often
# hundreds of megabytes.
.ir.json
*.sarif
.sast-baseline.json

# Dependencies and caches
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Go build and test output
*.test
*.out

# The raw CWE release: ~18MB, downloadable from cwe.mitre.org, and reproducible.
# What the build actually embeds is the generated cwe.json, which IS committed:
# without it the ledger cannot compile.
catalog/cwec_v*.xml

# The review loop's own working files.
#
# This is how the engine was validated, not part of what it is. The artifacts name which
# repositories were scanned, what was found in them, and where -- and a finding in somebody
# else's unfixed software is theirs to disclose, not ours. Eight drafted reports were pushed to
# this repository minutes after the same findings went to maintainers privately, which is what
# taught us to draw the line here rather than file by file.
#
# The method is public and lives in docs/review-loop.md, which describes the whole process and
# names no weakness in anybody's software. The harness that runs it stays local.
loop/

# The verdict ledger and the defect list, for the same reason as loop/.
#
# verdicts.json records 57 findings judged TRUE in named third-party repositories, each with the
# reasoning that makes it credible -- which is what makes it a disclosure. loop-issues.md quotes
# real code from those repositories while explaining what the engine could not see.
#
# Both are how the engine was validated rather than what it is. validation/README.md explains
# what the ledger does and why a verdict recorded once is never re-asked; the judgements
# themselves stay local.
validation/verdicts.json
loop-issues.md
